The growing deployment of autonomous platforms, aerospace systems, industrial robotics and advanced navigation technologies is increasing the need for reliable motion sensing and orientation measurement. Inertial measurement units (IMUs) are essential components in these systems because they measure motion-related parameters and help determine how a device moves and changes orientation.

According to SemiconductorInsight, the Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) Market was valued at USD 3.836 billion in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 7.319 billion by 2034, representing a CAGR of 7.4% during the 2026–2034 forecast period. The report identifies North America as the leading regional market in 2025.

An IMU typically combines accelerometers and gyroscopes to measure linear acceleration and angular velocity. Depending on the design, it may incorporate additional sensing capabilities or operate alongside satellite navigation, cameras, LiDAR and other positioning technologies. These measurements support motion tracking, stabilization, navigation and control.

