Why Are IMUs Essential to Modern Navigation?

Many electronic systems need to estimate movement even when external positioning signals are unavailable, interrupted or unreliable. IMUs provide motion measurements that can be processed to estimate orientation, movement and changes in position.

In navigation systems, inertial measurements can complement GPS or other satellite-based positioning. In robotics, they help control movement and support balance or stabilization. Aerospace applications use inertial sensing in systems that require dependable orientation and motion information.

The performance requirements vary substantially by application. Consumer electronics may prioritize compact size and low power consumption, while aerospace and defense systems can require higher stability, precision and rigorous qualification.

Robotics and Autonomous Systems Create New Opportunities

Robotics is expanding into industrial automation, logistics, inspection, service applications and other specialized environments. Robots must estimate their motion and orientation to perform tasks accurately, making inertial sensing a useful component of their control and perception systems.

Autonomous vehicles and mobile platforms can combine IMU measurements with cameras, satellite navigation and other sensors to improve state estimation. Such systems may use sensor fusion to compensate for the limitations of any individual sensing technology.

Emerging applications involving humanoid robots, low-altitude aircraft and advanced autonomous platforms could create additional demand, although adoption depends on performance requirements, system cost and the maturity of the end market.

MEMS Technology and High-Precision Sensing

Microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) have enabled the development of compact inertial sensors suited to high-volume electronic products and embedded systems. MEMS-based IMUs can offer small form factors and integration advantages for applications with space and power constraints.

Higher-performance applications may require more sophisticated sensing architectures, calibration, thermal compensation and signal processing. Fiber-optic gyroscopes and other precision technologies are relevant in applications where stability and navigation accuracy are particularly important.

The appropriate technology depends on the required accuracy, operating environment, cost target and system architecture.

