Sensor Fusion and Integration Influence Product Development

IMUs are increasingly incorporated into systems that combine multiple sensing modalities. Fusing inertial data with camera, LiDAR, GNSS or other measurements can improve motion estimation and support more robust navigation.

Integration creates additional engineering requirements involving calibration, synchronization, noise management, drift compensation and software processing. The IMU's performance must be evaluated as part of the overall system rather than solely through its standalone specifications.

Market Challenges and Qualification Requirements

Accuracy, bias stability, measurement noise, vibration tolerance, temperature performance and long-term reliability can affect IMU selection. High-performance units may require more expensive components and extensive testing, while lower-cost designs must balance performance against power consumption and size.

Aerospace and defense applications can also involve lengthy qualification and procurement cycles. For equipment manufacturers, supply continuity and long-term product support are important because replacing a sensing component may require system-level validation.

